--- Deflated Sharpe Ratio sensitivity grid, exploratory historical walk-forward period, net of 5 bps costs ---
No single (sharpe_variance_across_trials, n_trials) pair is treated as primary -- Lopez de Prado & Porcu (2026) frame search-adjusted significance as depending jointly on effective trial count AND cross-trial dispersion, and recommend reporting a disclosed range over defensible assumptions when dependence among candidates (here, several trials are sequential refinements of the same underlying run) cannot be estimated directly -- which is the case here since no daily return path survives for any historical trial. See outputs/trial_candidate_sharpes.csv for the recovered per-candidate Sharpes and evaluation.build_dsr_sensitivity_scenarios for how the grid below is assembled.

Variance scenarios (cross-trial Sharpe variance):
  pooled_deduped_all_trials: 0.0345
  pooled_including_redundant_rows: 0.0304
  pooled_clean_sample_only: 0.0394
  family_ad_hoc_pre_calendar_blend: 0.0390
  family_clean_validation_only_2015_2018: 0.0000
  family_clean_final_test_prespecified_secondary_check: 0.0116
  conventional_unit_variance: 1.0000
K scenarios (effective number of trials):
  literal_logged_trials: 7
  deduped_candidate_count: 9
  coherent_search_families: 3
Basis note: the observed Sharpes graded below are in excess of the T-bill rate, but the variance scenarios come from outputs/trial_candidate_sharpes.csv, recovered from commit messages and earlier CSVs on a zero-risk-free basis with no daily return paths to recompute them. DSR uses only the cross-trial VARIANCE, which a common shift leaves unchanged; candidates evaluated over different periods or volatilities would shift by different amounts, so read the variance scenarios as approximate on the excess basis.
Each cell reports three representations side by side: DSR-L (deflated_sharpe_ratio, the original Bailey & Lopez de Prado 2014 formulation -- search-adjusted location mu_K plus the *observed series' own* skew/kurtosis-adjusted sampling SE s_c); DSR-LS,Gaussian (deflated_sharpe_ratio_ls_gaussian -- search-adjusted location AND scale (mu_K, sigma_K) under an iid Gaussian candidate-search model, Gaussian reference shape, no skew/kurtosis adjustment since sigma_K is a search-distribution property, not s_c); and DSR-LS,Gumbel (deflated_sharpe_ratio_ls_gumbel -- identical (mu_K, sigma_K), but the EVT-consistent standardized-Gumbel reference shape for this same Gaussian search model, per Lopez de Prado & Porcu 2026's calibration result that the Gaussian reference's limiting rejection rate is 6.583% against a nominal 5%, while the Gumbel reference is asymptotically exact). All three are different representations of the same search-adjusted null, not competing estimates.
erc_baseline: net-of-cost Sharpe=0.455, DSR-L range=[0.002, 0.893], DSR-LS,Gaussian range=[0.043, 1.000], DSR-LS,Gumbel range=[0.006, 1.000] across 7x3 assumption grid
erc_regime: net-of-cost Sharpe=0.455, DSR-L range=[0.002, 0.893], DSR-LS,Gaussian range=[0.042, 1.000], DSR-LS,Gumbel range=[0.006, 1.000] across 7x3 assumption grid
erc_blend: net-of-cost Sharpe=0.458, DSR-L range=[0.002, 0.895], DSR-LS,Gaussian range=[0.043, 1.000], DSR-LS,Gumbel range=[0.006, 1.000] across 7x3 assumption grid
equal_weight: net-of-cost Sharpe=0.608, DSR-L range=[0.007, 0.950], DSR-LS,Gaussian range=[0.071, 1.000], DSR-LS,Gumbel range=[0.025, 1.000] across 7x3 assumption grid
lstm_baseline: net-of-cost Sharpe=0.540, DSR-L range=[0.004, 0.930], DSR-LS,Gaussian range=[0.057, 1.000], DSR-LS,Gumbel range=[0.014, 1.000] across 7x3 assumption grid
lstm_regime: net-of-cost Sharpe=0.440, DSR-L range=[0.002, 0.885], DSR-LS,Gaussian range=[0.040, 1.000], DSR-LS,Gumbel range=[0.005, 1.000] across 7x3 assumption grid
lstm_blend: net-of-cost Sharpe=0.448, DSR-L range=[0.002, 0.890], DSR-LS,Gaussian range=[0.041, 1.000], DSR-LS,Gumbel range=[0.005, 1.000] across 7x3 assumption grid
Saved full grid to /content/capstone_m6_final/outputs/real_data_dsr_sensitivity_grid.csv
Compare each config's printed ranges above against the variance/K scenarios: on the archived reference run, every recovered empirical-variance scenario (pooled or per-family) lands far above the conventional unit-variance scenario for ALL THREE representations, regardless of which K or reference shape is used -- a wide, config-independent swing driven almost entirely by the variance assumption, not by the choice between DSR-L/DSR-LS or between Gaussian/Gumbel. If that pattern holds here too, the DSR conclusion is NOT stable across this grid, which is itself the reportable finding, not any single cell.

